# generic_forward_chain_deep.tcyr — 6.7.16: a chain of 200 generics forwarding their T, called at f64, compiles and runs
# on every host's own compiler (issues/archived/2026-10-18-generic-forwarding-chain-hits-instance-nesting-cap.md).
#
# An instance is parsed when a call first reaches it, nested inside that call, so the chain below instantiates o199$f64
# inside main's call, o198$f64 inside o199$f64's, and so on to the bounded `describe`. Two compiler limits refused it:
# the parser's nesting bound (256 levels, two per link: 122 links were "nesting too deep (over 256 levels)") — the
# instance's body now counts its own levels, and the stack itself bounds the chain (6 MB, ~300 links; the stack and the
# frames differ per host, which is why this runs on all four) — and on x86 (Linux, PE, Intel macOS) the enclosing fn's
# jump-target table (1023), which held every nested instance's targets: ~190 links, or ~210 distinct generics called
# from one fn (W below), were "function exceeds 1023 jump targets (x86 LASE cap)". An instance's code is now opaque to
# the fns around it and its targets stay its own. The refusals past the stack are
# tests/gates/frontend/trait_bounds_checked.sh F14d-F14h and tests/gates/diagnostics/recursion_depth_bounded.sh.
include "lib/syscalls.cyr"
include "lib/alloc.cyr"
include "lib/assert.cyr"

trait Show { fn show(self): i64; }
fn describe<T: Show>(x: T): i64 { return x.show(); }
impl Show for f64 { fn show(self): i64 { return 5; } }

